WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.649 --> 00:00:11.209
Malcolm Grant: May we move onto the next item,
which is our finance report. Paul?

00:00:11.209 --> 00:00:16.630
Thank you, Malcolm. Last time we met, I was
able to give you an update not just on the

00:00:16.630 --> 00:00:21.910
month-ten numbers which were in the formal
written report, but on the month-11 data that

00:00:21.910 --> 00:00:28.310
we had just a day or two before we met as
a Board. This indicated last time a range

00:00:28.310 --> 00:00:36.080
of underspends between £494 million and £525
million for 2015/2016, the year just completed.

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:40.680
It is important to remember, while that is
a big number in pound terms, that is about

00:00:40.690 --> 00:00:46.750
0.5% of the money that we are accountable
for as an organisation.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:51.580
Since we last met, the finance teams up and
down the country have been beavering away

00:00:51.589 --> 00:00:56.350
to draw up the final scores. I am very pleased
to say that we have had an extremely smooth

00:00:56.350 --> 00:01:02.430
process here again in terms of the delivery
of that, which is of course a great tribute

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:09.369
to the professionalism of the teams in CCGs,
in CSUs and, indeed, in NHS England. A full

00:01:09.369 --> 00:01:16.020
set of consolidated accounts is now with the
National Audit Office, who are busily auditing

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:22.659
them in their usual fashion. In the event,
some of the risks that I talked about in month

00:01:22.659 --> 00:01:28.670
11 did not actually materialise as we drew
up the final scores, and also the technical

00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:33.130
aspects of our financial reporting to central
government, where we have to strip out provision

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:38.580
movements and obligation for reporting against
the core reporting metric; those adjustments

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:43.630
were slightly more positive than we had anticipated.
So the draft accounts that we have submitted

00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:49.710
to the NAO show an overall underspend for
the commissioning sector against the general

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:55.659
RDEL limit of £599&nbsp;million. Apologies for
Marks and Spencer’s pricing, but there we

00:01:55.659 --> 00:01:59.510
go. £599 million, almost £600 million.

00:01:59.510 --> 00:02:05.220
Within this, I have drawn out three broad
themes in my report today. Firstly, the operational

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:09.539
performance of the system – the operational
financial performance, I mean – was almost

00:02:09.539 --> 00:02:13.840
exactly in line with the plan overall; in
terms of both activity, which we have been

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:20.069
hearing about, and also the money as it then
expresses itself in operational performance.

00:02:20.069 --> 00:02:27.549
So the final analysis: we have a slight underspend
on specialised commissioning, which has been

00:02:27.549 --> 00:02:32.620
commented on already by John. It is a great
achievement, I think, of the specialised commissioning

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:40.739
teams responsible for that. Also a slight
overspend on CCGs which, again, is within

00:02:40.739 --> 00:02:46.769
the roundings; 0.1%, I think, or thereabouts,
of their total resource. That is the operational

00:02:46.769 --> 00:02:47.590
performance.

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:51.549
The second thing to draw your attention to
is the Cancer Drugs Fund, which overspent

00:02:51.549 --> 00:02:57.430
again by £126 million. That should be the
last time I am required to say those words

00:02:57.430 --> 00:03:01.680
to you, because of course the measures that
we decided upon a meeting or two back, which

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:08.959
will take effect in July, will mean we have
effective price-setting mechanisms which will

00:03:08.959 --> 00:03:16.059
prevent further overspends from taking resources
from other areas into that particular area,

00:03:16.059 --> 00:03:21.269
and will contain our spending on the Cancer
Drugs Fund to the £340 million we set aside

00:03:21.269 --> 00:03:22.560
for that purpose.

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:26.900
Then thirdly, the rest of the underspend that
I have been talking about concerns a variety

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:32.159
of mostly centrally managed budgets, where
we have a combination of slippage, managed

00:03:32.159 --> 00:03:37.590
underspend and once-off benefits. I probably
just need to repeat my words of caution from

00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:41.569
last time – indeed, I think I might be getting
boring and repetitive in saying this every

00:03:41.569 --> 00:03:46.980
time we meet – which were really threefold.
Firstly, the once-off benefits, particularly

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:50.879
in transition costs on some of the budgets
that we manage on behalf of the Department

00:03:50.879 --> 00:03:57.199
of Health, are just that: once-off. Where
we have had lower spending which can be expected

00:03:57.199 --> 00:04:01.989
to continue, in some directorate budgets and
in some legacy continuing healthcare claims,

00:04:01.989 --> 00:04:07.480
we have already reduced the budgets, as you
will recall, in 2016/2017, to reflect that,

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:13.419
and to maximise the amount of resource invested
in front‑line services and transformation.

00:04:13.419 --> 00:04:18.230
Thirdly, where we have had slippage on some
of the transformation programmes, in particular,

00:04:18.230 --> 00:04:22.819
the ones that started up during 2015/2016,
we will generally need to make this up in

00:04:22.819 --> 00:04:29.990
2017 if progress is going to be sustained
towards our Five Year Forward View. So it

00:04:29.990 --> 00:04:34.580
would be extremely unwise, I think, to extrapolate
the underspends that we have just been reporting

00:04:34.580 --> 00:04:41.289
for 2015/2016 into the future. Nonetheless,
the underspend that we have recorded in the

00:04:41.289 --> 00:04:46.199
commissioning sector, small as it is in relation
to our £101 billion budget, is obviously

00:04:46.199 --> 00:04:51.550
welcome in its own right, and in particular
as a contribution to the overall health sector

00:04:51.550 --> 00:04:52.370
position.

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:57.560
If I take you on a trip to the other side
of London, where there is another board meeting

00:04:57.560 --> 00:05:02.620
going on at this very moment, of course, the
numbers for the provider sector are being

00:05:02.620 --> 00:05:07.009
reported there; you will probably have taken
the time to read the extensive commentary

00:05:07.009 --> 00:05:13.979
on that which NHS Improvement has put together.
The numbers are problematic: the deficit has

00:05:13.979 --> 00:05:24.370
grown from £804 million in 2014/2015 to £2.45
billion in 2015/2016. As their commentary

00:05:24.370 --> 00:05:30.039
makes clear, there has been significant revenue
growth; there has been 2% revenue growth year

00:05:30.039 --> 00:05:36.509
on year, equivalent to about £1.4 billion.
The issue is that there has also been expenditure

00:05:36.509 --> 00:05:42.329
growth, which has been significantly ahead
of that revenue growth, at £3 billion, or

00:05:42.329 --> 00:05:50.599
4%. The combination of 4% cost growth and
2% revenue growth is the underlying factor

00:05:50.599 --> 00:05:53.659
in this.
Of that cost increase, the single factor is,

00:05:53.660 --> 00:05:58.639
again – I think it has been widely reported
– the issue of agency cost, which rather

00:05:58.639 --> 00:06:03.629
than going down by £900 million, which we
planned for the year, went up by a further

00:06:03.629 --> 00:06:13.039
just over £500 million, and that combination
of things caused the bulk of the cost problem

00:06:13.039 --> 00:06:17.979
that they have been reporting this year. Clearly,
that is the main thing that is going to need

00:06:17.979 --> 00:06:22.219
to be addressed as we go into 2016/2017 – and
I will come back to that in a minute – in

00:06:22.219 --> 00:06:27.819
order to ensure that we get back to the sort
of balance that we need across the sector.

00:06:27.819 --> 00:06:37.020
For now at least, £2.45 billion of deficit
in the provider sector.

00:06:37.020 --> 00:06:41.920
We will, of course, have to wait until the
Department of Health produces its consolidated

00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:47.440
accounts – and gets those audited by the
NAO – to know whether our contribution to

00:06:47.449 --> 00:06:51.860
that and the things which the Department of
Health has been doing will be sufficient to

00:06:51.860 --> 00:06:58.780
offset that deficit in the provider sector.
Obviously, I will update you on that, if you

00:06:58.780 --> 00:07:05.699
do not read it in the accounts which are put
in by our department in July, to see the answer

00:07:05.699 --> 00:07:10.900
to that. That is 2015/2016. I will happily
take questions on that at the end.

00:07:10.900 --> 00:07:14.090
Having dealt with last year, I thought it
might be worth spending a couple of moments

00:07:14.090 --> 00:07:20.710
updating you on the present preoccupation
we have with creating robust plans for 2016/2017,

00:07:20.710 --> 00:07:24.569
which I would remind you is the critical year
of transition, where we need to be able to

00:07:24.569 --> 00:07:29.849
use the £3.8&nbsp;billion real-terms increase
in funding to create both financial stability

00:07:29.849 --> 00:07:36.370
and balance across the whole sector; but also
to pick up the pace of transformation through

00:07:36.370 --> 00:07:40.999
the programmes that we have discussed many
times. Achieving that balance is proving,

00:07:40.999 --> 00:07:45.930
as you would expect, extremely challenging,
for three main reasons, which it is just worth

00:07:45.930 --> 00:07:47.810
reminding ourselves of.

00:07:47.810 --> 00:07:52.039
Firstly, as I have just said, the provider
sector is having to find ways to correct cost

00:07:52.039 --> 00:07:57.909
overrun, which led to the underlying deficit
coming forward from 2015/2016, significantly

00:07:57.909 --> 00:08:04.419
larger than sustainability funding of £1.8
billion included in the spending review package.

00:08:04.419 --> 00:08:05.909
That is the first point.

00:08:05.909 --> 00:08:10.849
The second point is, given that all the available
funding has been channelled into the NHS,

00:08:10.849 --> 00:08:16.169
quite rightly, in the spending review, we
are having to ensure that we have enough flexibility

00:08:16.169 --> 00:08:20.860
within our own resources within the NHS to
deal with any risks which emerge during the

00:08:20.860 --> 00:08:25.650
course of the year. In particular, that has
required commissioners to create a risk reserve,

00:08:25.650 --> 00:08:30.719
effectively, of 1% within their budgets, which
has in turn required commissioners to draw

00:08:30.719 --> 00:08:35.900
up plans for significantly higher levels of
efficiency gain. Here I am talking about the

00:08:35.900 --> 00:08:41.450
efficiency created by commissioners through
reducing demand for activity, or by commissioning

00:08:41.450 --> 00:08:46.070
more cost-effective ways of delivering care,
as opposed to the operational efficiencies

00:08:46.070 --> 00:08:54.000
which providers are accountable for. In 2016/2017,
commissioner efficiencies, within that definition,

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:58.460
will need to run at about 3% compared with
this year’s, where it has been closer to

00:08:58.460 --> 00:09:04.120
2%. So clearly, that is a big concentrating
factor in plans of commissioners.

00:09:04.120 --> 00:09:08.530
Finally, of course, we have been working hard
to ensure that enough activity is commissioned

00:09:08.530 --> 00:09:15.230
in 2016/2017, and funded, to clear the operational
backlogs which Richard has talked about already

00:09:15.230 --> 00:09:19.990
in the context of the performance report,
and ensure that we are on an even keel going

00:09:19.990 --> 00:09:26.000
forward. Three significant reasons why this
is a challenging context in which to plan.

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:31.180
I am pleased to report that in the last few
weeks, we have made some rapid progress towards

00:09:31.180 --> 00:09:37.190
resolving, in particular, the contracting
round; almost all of the contracts are now

00:09:37.190 --> 00:09:42.550
at a point where the terms have been agreed.
Many of them have been signed; it always takes

00:09:42.550 --> 00:09:46.870
a little bit longer. We have been through
the process of quite a small number of places

00:09:46.870 --> 00:09:52.780
which needed external arbitration support
to resolve issues, and are now at the point

00:09:52.780 --> 00:09:59.470
where most contracts are ready to be acted
upon, and most organisations have their plans

00:09:59.470 --> 00:10:05.980
ready to start from.
Briefly, at the final submission a week ago

00:10:05.980 --> 00:10:12.260
or so, the CCGs and NHS England operating
plans show an overall balanced position, which

00:10:12.260 --> 00:10:17.460
is where it needs to be, with the 1% risk
reserve I talked about set aside as planned;

00:10:17.460 --> 00:10:21.980
but I have to say, with a level of risk, particularly
with regard to the delivery of that increased

00:10:21.980 --> 00:10:28.090
level of efficiency, which is significantly
higher than in previous years, and will require

00:10:28.090 --> 00:10:33.530
us to build on the vigilance of our monitoring
which we have been honing over the last few

00:10:33.530 --> 00:10:39.620
years, and in particular, the speed of our
support and intervention where required, where

00:10:39.620 --> 00:10:47.240
individual organisations start to show signs
of deviation from those plans. That is the

00:10:47.240 --> 00:10:48.580
commissioner position.

00:10:48.580 --> 00:10:53.790
NHS Improvement has also continued to work
with provider organisations to agree updated

00:10:53.790 --> 00:10:59.650
control totals and revise plans which deliver
the overall balance position which, as Simon

00:10:59.650 --> 00:11:04.860
mentioned earlier on, we need from the providers
to make the overall plans work. I am sure

00:11:04.860 --> 00:11:08.640
they will be reporting on that at their own
board meeting, but you will not be surprised

00:11:08.640 --> 00:11:12.790
to hear that there is still quite a bit of
work to do with a number of organisations

00:11:12.790 --> 00:11:20.560
to secure the efficiencies required to achieve
that particular balance, and clearly a lot

00:11:20.560 --> 00:11:27.750
of that is tied up with the agency labour
cost inflation that we discussed in the context

00:11:27.750 --> 00:11:30.030
of 2015/2016.

00:11:30.030 --> 00:11:33.370
There is, of course, a degree of urgency now
to the task of putting all of that behind

00:11:33.370 --> 00:11:37.460
us and getting on with it, because clearly
the challenge in 2016/2017 is not so much

00:11:37.460 --> 00:11:42.250
creating the plans and delivering the productivity
gains, both the operational productivity gains

00:11:42.250 --> 00:11:47.390
and the commissioner-driven efficiencies that
I have been talking about. Now that we have

00:11:47.390 --> 00:11:52.520
much of the planning behind us, we need obviously
to concentrate all of our efforts on delivering

00:11:52.520 --> 00:11:57.650
a set of plans which are critical to the future
of the NHS and will be challenging to deliver,

00:11:57.650 --> 00:12:02.400
and will require all the resilience that we
have attempted to build up in the system in

00:12:02.400 --> 00:12:08.780
recent times. On that note, perhaps I will
stop and invite any questions, comments or

00:12:08.780 --> 00:12:10.870
contributions.

00:12:16.260 --> 00:12:23.160
David Roberts: I think, Malcolm, just for
the Board, the Audit Committee has had an

00:12:23.170 --> 00:12:29.770
ongoing discussion with Paul through the year
on the performance, the outturn, the likely

00:12:29.770 --> 00:12:37.330
outturn, the risks. I think at this point,
it is worth commending Paul and the finance

00:12:37.330 --> 00:12:47.810
team for their ability to predict accurately
where we might land. That is no mean feat

00:12:47.810 --> 00:12:55.350
on a £101 billion budget.
The second thing I would say is that we are

00:12:55.350 --> 00:13:02.420
all going through the audit process. We have
had conversations with the NAO, we will undoubtedly

00:13:02.420 --> 00:13:09.720
have more conversations, but that process
is proceeding on track, and pretty smoothly.

00:13:11.340 --> 00:13:17.000
The third thing I would say is that past performance
should not be seen as a predictor of future

00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:29.040
success, and the naturee of the performance
and the underspend in 2015/2016 of £599 million

00:13:29.050 --> 00:13:36.000
is predominantly one-off in nature, just to
reiterate what Paul said. In my judgement,

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:43.560
the degree of risk that we now have within
the 2016/2017 position is materially higher,

00:13:43.560 --> 00:13:50.070
and the levers to manage that risk are less
available to us. Therefore, it is important

00:13:50.070 --> 00:13:59.050
– it becomes really key – to Paul’s
comment that we have good intelligence as

00:13:59.050 --> 00:14:09.120
to what is moving, what is happening; that
we have clear, planned support and intervention

00:14:09.120 --> 00:14:18.880
both in the commissioner side and in conjunction
with NHSI; and that we move rapidly where

00:14:18.880 --> 00:14:27.380
we see challenges to support people.
3% productivity improvement on the commissioners’

00:14:27.380 --> 00:14:35.050
side is a material ask, at a time when, as
we all know, the service has many challenges.

00:14:35.050 --> 00:14:42.610
So I think we should be confident that we
have done this before. I think we should be

00:14:42.610 --> 00:14:49.400
thoughtful about the scale of risk, and we
should be aware that we will have to strain

00:14:49.400 --> 00:14:58.880
every sinew to deliver. The team are in a
good place to do that, but we will, I suspect,

00:14:58.880 --> 00:15:01.750
be spending more time on this challenge in
2016/2017 than we have been in 2015/2016,

00:15:01.750 --> 00:15:09.650
as the position is just tougher.
Malcolm Grant: Thank you, David, for that.

00:15:12.610 --> 00:15:20.750
Malcolm Grant: I think that has probably dissuaded us from coming in at that point; it is a salient reminder

00:15:20.750 --> 00:15:26.240
to us all of the turbulent journey that lies
ahead. Thank you, Paul, and thank you David

00:15:26.240 --> 00:15:27.980
for that closing comment.

